\description{
Get the play-by-play for a single drive of a college football
game -- one row per play, scoped to one drive.
}
\details{
Wraps the ESPN core-v2 endpoint
\code{events/{game_id}/competitions/{game_id}/drives/{drive_id}/plays}. The
competition id always equals the game id. Returns the same play schema as
the competition-level \code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_pbp]{espn_cfb_game_pbp()}} feed, but partitioned to a
single drive (typically 5-11 plays). Harvest the \code{drive_id} from
\code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_drives]{espn_cfb_game_drives()}} -- its \code{drive_id} column lists every drive in the
game.

When \code{participants = "wide"}, the base play schema is kept intact and
six columns are appended for every participant \code{type} that appears
anywhere in the drive. The column names are \strong{dynamic} -- one set per
participant type present (e.g. \code{passer}, \code{rusher}, \code{receiver},
\code{tackler}, \code{sacked_by}, \code{pass_defender}, \code{kicker}, \code{returner}). ESPN's
camelCase types are snake-cased (\code{sackedBy} -> \code{sacked_by}). For each
type the appended columns are:
\itemize{
\item \verb{\{type\}_player_id} -- scalar character, the \strong{first}
occurrence of that type on the play (\code{NA} if none).
\item \verb{\{type\}_player_name} -- scalar character, that athlete's
roster-joined name (\code{NA} if none or unmatched).
\item \verb{\{type\}_player_position} -- scalar character, the first
athlete's position abbreviation (joined from the ESPN position
catalog; \code{NA} if unmatched).
\item \verb{\{type\}_player_position_name} -- scalar character, the first
athlete's position name.
\item \verb{\{type\}_player_ids} -- a \strong{list-column}: a character vector
of \strong{every} athlete id of that type on the play, in ESPN order.
Plays with none carry \code{character(0)}.
\item \verb{\{type\}_player_names} -- a \strong{list-column}: the parallel
vector of roster-joined names.
}

Participant \code{stats[]} are not carried in wide mode -- use \code{"long"} for
the per-participant stat lines.

When \code{participants = "long"}, the frame is expanded to one row per
play x participant with \code{participant_index}, \code{participant_athlete_id},
\code{participant_type}, \code{participant_order}, the roster-joined
\code{participant_athlete_name} / \code{participant_position} /
\code{participant_jersey} / \code{participant_team_id}, and the participant's
stats pivoted to named \verb{pstat_<statname>} columns. Plays with zero
participants still yield one row (participant fields \code{NA}).

When \code{participants_list = TRUE}, a single list-column named
\code{participants} is appended. Each cell is a tibble with one row per
participant on that play and columns \code{participant_index} (1-based, ESPN
order), \code{type} (snake_cased, e.g. \code{sacked_by}, \code{pass_defender}),
\code{athlete_id}, \code{athlete_name} (roster-joined), \code{order}, \code{position_id},
plus one column per participant \code{stat[]} name (\code{dplyr::bind_rows}
NA-fills participants whose stat sets differ). Plays with no
participants carry an empty 0-row tibble, never \code{NULL}. This option is
independent of \code{participants} and composes with any of its modes -- e.g.
\verb{participants = "wide", participants_list = TRUE} yields the type-keyed
\verb{\{type\}_player_*} columns \strong{and} the \code{participants} list-column, while
\verb{participants = "none", participants_list = TRUE} adds only the
list-column. With \code{participants = "long"} the list-column repeats per
play x participant row. The raw plays are fetched only once even when
both options are active.

When \code{team_participants = "wide"}, three columns are appended for every
\code{teamParticipants[]} \code{type} present anywhere in the drive (ESPN ships
\code{offense} / \code{defense}). The column names are \strong{dynamic} -- one set per
type, snake-cased: \verb{\{type\}_team_id} (scalar character, the first
occurrence of that type on the play), \verb{\{type\}_team_order} (scalar
integer, ESPN's display order), and \verb{\{type\}_team_ref} (scalar
character, the \verb{$ref} URL to that team resource). Plays with none of a
type carry \code{NA}.

When \code{team_participants_list = TRUE}, a single list-column named
\code{team_participants} is appended. Each cell is a tibble with one row per
team credited on the play and columns \code{team_participant_index}
(1-based, ESPN order), \code{team_id}, \code{team_ref}, \code{order}, and \code{type}
(e.g. \code{offense}, \code{defense}). A play with no team participants carries
an empty 0-row tibble, never \code{NULL}. This option is independent of
\code{team_participants} and composes with it -- e.g.
\verb{team_participants = "wide", team_participants_list = TRUE} yields both
the type-keyed \verb{\{type\}_team_*} columns \strong{and} the \code{team_participants}
list-column, while \code{team_participants = "none"} adds neither.

When \code{team_detail = TRUE} (the default), the ESPN team catalog
(\code{\link[=espn_cfb_teams]{espn_cfb_teams()}}) is fetched once and joined onto \strong{every} team-id
column the output carries -- the base \code{team_id} / \code{start_team_id} /
\code{end_team_id}, plus any optional \verb{\{type\}_team_id} (from
\code{team_participants = "wide"}) and \verb{\{type\}_player_team_id} (from
\code{participants = "long"}) columns. For each id column \code{X_id} the friendly
siblings \code{X_name}, \code{X_abbreviation}, \code{X_location}, \code{X_display_name},
\code{X_short_display_name}, \code{X_nickname}, \code{X_color}, \code{X_alternate_color},
\code{X_logo_href}, and \code{X_logo_dark_href} are inserted
immediately after it (e.g. \code{team_id} -> \code{team_name},
\code{team_abbreviation}, ...; \code{start_team_id} -> \code{start_team_name}, ...).
Rows whose id is missing or unmatched receive \code{NA}, and a catalog-fetch
failure degrades the whole set to \code{NA} rather than erroring the wrapper.
With \code{team_detail = FALSE} the friendly columns (and the catalog fetch)
are skipped.
}
